WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.528 --> 00:00:03.759
- I waited a long time out in the world

00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:06.519
before I gave myself permission to fail.

00:00:06.519 --> 00:00:08.418
You need a time in your
life right after college

00:00:08.418 --> 00:00:10.850
to suffer a little bit
to keep you on your toes.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.430
I think it's not so
good to live in the past

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.119
or have the clarity of the past.

00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:17.452
I'm guilty of being a dwarf.

00:00:18.498 --> 00:00:19.348
- Hello, Believe Nation.

00:00:19.348 --> 00:00:20.489
I'm Evan Carmichael.

00:00:20.489 --> 00:00:22.030
My one word is believe

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:23.959
and I believe that entrepreneurs are going

00:00:23.959 --> 00:00:26.970
to solve all of the major
problems of the world.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.859
So to help you on your journey,
today we're going to learn

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:31.007
from Game of Thrones
actor, Peter Dinklage,

00:00:31.007 --> 00:00:34.174
and my take on his top
ten rules for success.

00:00:34.174 --> 00:00:35.785
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:35.785 --> 00:00:38.125
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:38.125 --> 00:00:39.607
And as always, guys, as you're watching,

00:00:39.607 --> 00:00:41.738
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:41.738 --> 00:00:43.856
please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:00:43.856 --> 00:00:46.068
Put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired

00:00:46.068 --> 00:00:47.825
and when you write it down,

00:00:47.825 --> 00:00:51.223
it's much more likely to
stick for yourself, as well.

00:00:51.223 --> 00:00:52.056
Enjoy.

00:00:55.056 --> 00:00:57.889
(uplifting music)

00:01:05.647 --> 00:01:09.814
- When I was 29, I told myself
the next acting job I get,

00:01:11.578 --> 00:01:14.767
no matter what it pays,
I will, from now on,

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:18.100
for better or worse, be a working actor.

00:01:20.335 --> 00:01:22.126
So I quit my position at

00:01:22.126 --> 00:01:23.989
the Professional Examination Services.

00:01:23.989 --> 00:01:25.345
(audience laughing)

00:01:25.345 --> 00:01:27.095
My friends really weren't happy about that

00:01:27.095 --> 00:01:29.858
because it was so easy to
find me when I worked there.

00:01:29.858 --> 00:01:32.180
Work was the only place
that I had the internet.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:33.628
This was at the beginning of the internet.

00:01:33.628 --> 00:01:34.539
(audience laughing)

00:01:34.539 --> 00:01:37.198
And now I didn't have
the either the internet

00:01:37.198 --> 00:01:39.787
or a cell phone or a job.

00:01:39.787 --> 00:01:41.128
(audience laughing)

00:01:41.128 --> 00:01:43.778
But something good happened.

00:01:43.778 --> 00:01:45.429
I got a low-paying theater job

00:01:45.429 --> 00:01:48.399
in a play called Imperfect Love,

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:53.395
which led to a film called 13
Moons with the same writer,

00:01:53.395 --> 00:01:56.868
which led to other roles,
which led to other roles.

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:59.988
And I've worked as an actor ever since.

00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:02.988
(audience cheering)

00:02:06.978 --> 00:02:09.978
But I didn't know that would happen.

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:15.584
At 29, walking away from data processing,

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:18.652
I was terrified.

00:02:19.948 --> 00:02:22.149
Ten years in a place without heat,

00:02:22.149 --> 00:02:24.929
six years at a job I felt stuck in,

00:02:24.929 --> 00:02:28.047
maybe I was afraid of change.

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:28.880
Are you?

00:02:32.728 --> 00:02:35.695
My parents didn't have much money,

00:02:35.695 --> 00:02:40.208
but they struggled to send
me to the best schools.

00:02:40.208 --> 00:02:43.978
And one of the most important
things they did for me,

00:02:43.978 --> 00:02:47.958
and graduates, maybe you
don't want to hear this,

00:02:47.958 --> 00:02:51.458
is that once I graduated, I was on my own.

00:02:52.307 --> 00:02:54.050
Financially it was my turn.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:57.217
(audience applauding)

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:02.779
Parents are applauding, graduates are not.

00:03:04.249 --> 00:03:07.249
(audience laughing)

00:03:08.298 --> 00:03:12.108
But this made me very hungry, literally.

00:03:12.108 --> 00:03:14.800
(audience laughing)

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.800
I couldn't be lazy.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.967
Now I'm totally lazy, but
back then I couldn't be.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:25.931
And so at 29 and at very long last,

00:03:25.931 --> 00:03:27.989
I was in the company of
the actors, and writers,

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:31.150
and directors I had sought
out that first year,

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.483
that first day after school.

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:38.443
I was, I am, by their sides.

00:03:42.518 --> 00:03:45.851
Raise the rest of your life to meet you.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:51.386
Don't search for defining moments

00:03:51.386 --> 00:03:53.241
because they will never come.

00:03:53.241 --> 00:03:57.158
The world might say you
are not allowed to yet.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.170
I waited a long time out in the world

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:06.503
before I gave myself permission to fail.

00:04:08.987 --> 00:04:11.654
Please don't even bother asking,

00:04:14.919 --> 00:04:18.669
don't bother telling
the world you are ready.

00:04:19.565 --> 00:04:20.398
Show it.

00:04:21.659 --> 00:04:22.492
Do it.

00:04:25.978 --> 00:04:27.728
What did Beckett say?

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:33.487
Ever tried, ever failed, no matter.

00:04:35.847 --> 00:04:38.764
Try again, fail again, fail better.

00:04:41.138 --> 00:04:43.190
- I'm a writer so I'm kind of curious.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:45.249
What type of scripts do
you get coming your way

00:04:45.249 --> 00:04:46.747
that you're tired of?

00:04:46.747 --> 00:04:50.274
- Originality, I know that's a very,

00:04:50.274 --> 00:04:53.895
but without it being aware
that it's being original.

00:04:53.895 --> 00:04:56.986
A lot of scripts are
trying to be very original

00:04:56.986 --> 00:04:58.234
and that they're so self-aware.

00:04:58.234 --> 00:05:02.446
I think we're living
nowadays in a meta society

00:05:02.446 --> 00:05:03.964
and we can't help ourselves in that.

00:05:03.964 --> 00:05:05.705
And I think a lot of it manifests a lot

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:07.622
in scripts that I read.

00:05:09.089 --> 00:05:11.871
Like they're, Charlie
Kaufman, for example,

00:05:11.871 --> 00:05:14.723
he came into this world
and he wrote those movies

00:05:14.723 --> 00:05:17.423
and since Charlie, people have tried

00:05:17.423 --> 00:05:19.573
to be like Charlie's movies, you know.

00:05:19.573 --> 00:05:21.663
They've sort of tried to follow that form.

00:05:21.663 --> 00:05:23.794
But it's not their own, it's Charlie's.

00:05:23.794 --> 00:05:28.323
And sort of follow your
own, take your own path,

00:05:28.323 --> 00:05:31.052
write what's true to you and no one else,

00:05:31.052 --> 00:05:33.714
and then it will be wholly original.

00:05:33.714 --> 00:05:35.100
That's what I look for.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:38.344
- When you grew older,
you moved to Brooklyn

00:05:38.344 --> 00:05:40.982
and in those days, Brooklyn was Brooklyn.

00:05:40.982 --> 00:05:42.311
- Yeah.
- It's different now.

00:05:42.311 --> 00:05:44.142
- Williamsburg in the early nineties.

00:05:44.142 --> 00:05:45.679
- What was your life like over there?

00:05:45.679 --> 00:05:46.563
- Terrible.
- Really?

00:05:46.563 --> 00:05:47.396
(audience laughing)

00:05:47.396 --> 00:05:48.229
- Yeah.

00:05:48.229 --> 00:05:51.092
Well, I think you need a time in your life

00:05:51.092 --> 00:05:52.861
right after college to suffer a little bit

00:05:52.861 --> 00:05:55.744
to keep you on your toes.

00:05:55.744 --> 00:05:58.733
But I remember I had this apartment

00:05:58.733 --> 00:06:00.020
that didn't have any heat.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:01.522
None of them ever had any heat back then.

00:06:01.522 --> 00:06:05.404
We put up plastic on the
windows in the winter.

00:06:05.404 --> 00:06:06.377
Sort of like in here.

00:06:06.377 --> 00:06:07.210
- [David Letterman] Yeah.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.831
(laughing)

00:06:09.831 --> 00:06:11.659
- I want to go into the future.

00:06:11.659 --> 00:06:13.999
I think this, I don't know
if I would use this machine

00:06:13.999 --> 00:06:15.661
or relive any memories.

00:06:15.661 --> 00:06:18.003
I think it's sort of, can be distractive.

00:06:18.003 --> 00:06:20.062
That's my take away from it.

00:06:20.062 --> 00:06:20.895
I don't know.

00:06:20.895 --> 00:06:21.728
You would.
- I would, yeah.

00:06:21.728 --> 00:06:22.900
I would use the machine, yeah.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:24.491
- I'm with you, I'm with you.

00:06:24.491 --> 00:06:25.822
- Yeah, it's just interesting, yeah.

00:06:25.822 --> 00:06:27.024
Would you use it?

00:06:27.024 --> 00:06:29.642
- I think it's not so
good to live in the past

00:06:29.642 --> 00:06:31.511
or have the clarity of the past.

00:06:31.511 --> 00:06:34.013
You should romanticize it.

00:06:34.013 --> 00:06:34.846
That's what we do.

00:06:34.846 --> 00:06:35.853
It's storytelling.

00:06:35.853 --> 00:06:37.242
- [Interviewer] But Peter,
this is the last season

00:06:37.242 --> 00:06:38.075
of Game of Thrones.

00:06:38.075 --> 00:06:39.533
Like, there's nothing you would miss?

00:06:39.533 --> 00:06:42.462
Or you have a favorite memory of that?

00:06:42.462 --> 00:06:44.062
- Ah, you got to move on.

00:06:44.062 --> 00:06:45.582
You got to move on!

00:06:45.582 --> 00:06:48.360
No, it's all about, it's all moving on.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.624
It's all about moving on.

00:06:50.624 --> 00:06:51.882
- You have people taking pictures,

00:06:51.882 --> 00:06:53.622
especially in a place like this.

00:06:53.622 --> 00:06:56.344
They just, they want
the proof of a memory,

00:06:56.344 --> 00:06:59.479
but they don't actually
experience something to remember.

00:06:59.479 --> 00:07:00.745
It's sort of strange,
"Can I have your picture?"

00:07:00.745 --> 00:07:01.813
And they don't really meet you

00:07:01.813 --> 00:07:04.204
or it's just this sort of
thing that happening now.

00:07:04.204 --> 00:07:08.744
It's replacing an experience,
a memory they will have.

00:07:08.744 --> 00:07:11.502
Instead it's just a memory of a photo.

00:07:11.502 --> 00:07:13.304
And I'm sure you start to apply

00:07:13.304 --> 00:07:15.132
your own memory to that photo

00:07:15.132 --> 00:07:18.633
and change the events
and how they went down.

00:07:18.633 --> 00:07:20.513
- What is the most important lesson

00:07:20.513 --> 00:07:24.214
that you have learned in
your course as professionals?

00:07:24.214 --> 00:07:27.044
- I think with the
culture we live in today,

00:07:27.044 --> 00:07:29.281
the internet and web, and
things, and the twitter,

00:07:29.281 --> 00:07:33.563
and the gobbler, and the (gibberish).

00:07:33.563 --> 00:07:38.020
You know, I think part of
the reason why Marlon Brando

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:40.122
is who Marlon Brando was, why he was,

00:07:40.122 --> 00:07:43.931
probably yes, to me,
the greatest actor ever,

00:07:43.931 --> 00:07:46.591
because we didn't really
know too much about him

00:07:46.591 --> 00:07:50.322
when he was younger and that
left it very mysterious.

00:07:50.322 --> 00:07:53.210
So we were able to get lost
in his characters more.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:55.581
But nowadays, there's so much knowledge

00:07:55.581 --> 00:07:58.382
of everybody's personal life
that you sort of can't get lost

00:07:58.382 --> 00:08:00.431
in the characters they play.

00:08:00.431 --> 00:08:03.731
So my professional thing that I've,

00:08:03.731 --> 00:08:06.731
is just to shut up and less is more.

00:08:08.901 --> 00:08:12.282
- Time and again we find that
we have an innermost trend

00:08:12.282 --> 00:08:13.461
of going back to basics.

00:08:13.461 --> 00:08:15.820
Because today people
complain that we have been

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.351
insulated for too long
by digital technology,

00:08:18.351 --> 00:08:19.962
too many special effects.

00:08:19.962 --> 00:08:21.402
We want to, you know,

00:08:21.402 --> 00:08:25.402
to have more real human
characters in the movie.

00:08:26.281 --> 00:08:27.231
What do you think?

00:08:27.231 --> 00:08:28.064
- Well, yeah.

00:08:28.064 --> 00:08:30.472
I mean, I kind of don't understand.

00:08:30.472 --> 00:08:34.377
I mean, films from the Far
East have always done it,

00:08:34.377 --> 00:08:37.960
that I have seen, why
can't an action movie

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:42.550
also be very heartbreaking and personal?

00:08:42.550 --> 00:08:45.967
Why can't a horror film be very dramatic?

00:08:46.827 --> 00:08:49.599
I feel like and sometimes
in America we get

00:08:49.599 --> 00:08:53.829
trapped by our genres
and we don't overlap.

00:08:53.829 --> 00:08:56.140
And I think that's unfortunate.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:57.399
And something like this,

00:08:57.399 --> 00:09:00.189
which has such science-fiction
elements to it,

00:09:00.189 --> 00:09:03.512
it also is a very deeply personal film

00:09:03.512 --> 00:09:04.936
for a lot of these characters

00:09:04.936 --> 00:09:09.019
and very dramatic and very
sad and heartbreaking.

00:09:11.678 --> 00:09:13.777
You get very emotional
watching a film like this.

00:09:13.777 --> 00:09:15.615
And you sort of forget
that you're watching

00:09:15.615 --> 00:09:17.646
a big summer blockbuster film.

00:09:17.646 --> 00:09:18.897
I think it's, I find it kind of sad that

00:09:18.897 --> 00:09:20.166
that's somewhat surprising

00:09:20.166 --> 00:09:23.028
because writing has gotten
so, sort of, formulaic.

00:09:23.028 --> 00:09:25.596
Especially, I think, sorry, in television.

00:09:25.596 --> 00:09:29.309
But you just got to push the envelope

00:09:29.309 --> 00:09:31.599
and challenge people's expectations

00:09:31.599 --> 00:09:35.016
and ideas of what's going to happen next.

00:09:36.269 --> 00:09:37.102
And I think we do that.

00:09:37.102 --> 00:09:41.080
And I think that adds to
the addiction of the show.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:42.729
You never know what's around the corner.

00:09:42.729 --> 00:09:44.562
Well, she's beautiful.

00:09:45.899 --> 00:09:49.638
All awards mean is that
it's the people are,

00:09:49.638 --> 00:09:50.560
it's just great.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:53.869
In other words, you know,
people loved what you did

00:09:53.869 --> 00:09:57.476
and that's always means
well, it means good.

00:09:57.476 --> 00:10:00.400
Actors, I think, need a lot
of confirmation (laughs)

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:01.363
for what they do.

00:10:01.363 --> 00:10:05.446
I think that's why we have
all these award shows.

00:10:06.616 --> 00:10:10.073
It's just a movie we made
for no money in 20 days

00:10:10.073 --> 00:10:13.062
and it just feels good to get recognized

00:10:13.062 --> 00:10:15.102
for something like that, you know.

00:10:15.102 --> 00:10:16.731
We made the movie for
half a million dollars

00:10:16.731 --> 00:10:19.213
and we struggled to get it made.

00:10:19.213 --> 00:10:21.463
And it's found an audience,

00:10:24.541 --> 00:10:26.091
which we didn't know it was going to find.

00:10:26.091 --> 00:10:27.738
You know, it's a very personal movie.

00:10:27.738 --> 00:10:30.553
Tom McCarthy, the writer
director, it's his first film

00:10:30.553 --> 00:10:34.119
and he wrote it for the three lead actors

00:10:34.119 --> 00:10:36.900
and then Miramax bought
the film at Sundance

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:38.400
about a year and a half ago.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:39.442
So it's been great.

00:10:39.442 --> 00:10:41.089
It's been a crazy ride.

00:10:41.089 --> 00:10:42.256
- I saved you.

00:10:44.121 --> 00:10:48.038
I saved this city and
all your worthless lives.

00:10:50.751 --> 00:10:53.870
I should have let Stannis kill you all.

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:54.791
(jeering)

00:10:54.791 --> 00:10:55.624
- Tyrion,

00:10:59.509 --> 00:11:01.426
do you wish to confess?

00:11:05.379 --> 00:11:07.546
- Yes, father, I'm guilty.

00:11:10.001 --> 00:11:13.440
Guilty, is that what you want to hear?

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.320
- You admit you poisoned the king?

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:18.270
- No, of that I'm innocent.

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:22.297
I'm guilty of a far more monstrous crime.

00:11:22.297 --> 00:11:24.630
I'm guilty of being a dwarf.

00:11:26.661 --> 00:11:29.069
- You are not on trial for being a dwarf.

00:11:29.069 --> 00:11:30.319
- Oh, yes I am.

00:11:31.471 --> 00:11:35.054
I've been on trial for
that my entire life.

00:11:36.721 --> 00:11:38.611
- Have you nothing to say in your defense?

00:11:38.611 --> 00:11:41.611
- Nothing but this, I did not do it.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:47.033
I did not kill Joffrey,
but I wish that I had.

00:11:48.297 --> 00:11:50.961
Watching your vicious bastard die

00:11:50.961 --> 00:11:55.044
gave me more relief than
a thousand lying whores.

00:11:58.089 --> 00:12:01.422
I wish I was the monster you think I am.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:07.711
I wish I had enough poison
for the whole pack of you.

00:12:07.711 --> 00:12:12.437
I would gladly give my life
to watch you all swallow it.

00:12:12.437 --> 00:12:14.219
- Sir Meryn, Sir Meryn,

00:12:14.219 --> 00:12:17.302
escort the prisoner back to his cell.

00:12:18.479 --> 00:12:23.089
- I will not give my
life for Joffrey's murder

00:12:23.089 --> 00:12:25.929
and I know I'll get no justice here.

00:12:25.929 --> 00:12:29.096
So I will let the gods decide my fate.

00:12:30.809 --> 00:12:33.059
I demand a trial by combat.

00:12:34.089 --> 00:12:35.700
(dramatic music)

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:38.367
(crowd yelling)

00:12:59.959 --> 00:13:01.579
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:01.579 --> 00:13:04.040
I made this video because
Roderick asked me to.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:05.229
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:05.229 --> 00:13:07.111
that you want me to profile next,

00:13:07.111 --> 00:13:08.430
check out the link in the description

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:10.112
and go and cast your vote.

00:13:10.112 --> 00:13:12.588
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Imran Dean.

00:13:12.588 --> 00:13:14.532
Imran, thank you so much
for picking up a copy

00:13:14.532 --> 00:13:15.951
of my book, Your One Word

00:13:15.951 --> 00:13:18.590
and posting that awesome
picture on Instagram.

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:20.338
I really, really appreciate the support

00:13:20.338 --> 00:13:22.081
and I'm glad that you enjoyed the read.

00:13:22.081 --> 00:13:23.631
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:13:23.631 --> 00:13:24.799
I believe in you.

00:13:24.799 --> 00:13:26.841
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:13:26.841 --> 00:13:29.001
and whatever your one word is.

00:13:29.001 --> 00:13:30.420
Much love.

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:31.920
I'll see you soon.

00:13:38.051 --> 00:13:39.013
- [Interviewer] Your
career, when I look at

00:13:39.013 --> 00:13:41.229
some of the roles you've had in the past,

00:13:41.229 --> 00:13:42.509
the break out role people
role people describe

00:13:42.509 --> 00:13:45.959
as being in the movie The
Station Agent a while back.

00:13:45.959 --> 00:13:47.679
Even a big budget film like Elf,

00:13:47.679 --> 00:13:51.119
where you played a very, sort of,

00:13:51.119 --> 00:13:53.330
pugnacious children's publisher.

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:55.500
If that is an accurate description.

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:56.333
- [Peter Dinklage] Yes.

00:13:56.333 --> 00:13:57.329
- [Interviewer] It seems
like you've managed

00:13:57.329 --> 00:13:59.341
to find the roles that upend

00:13:59.341 --> 00:14:02.220
whatever those stereotypes might be.

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:04.019
- [Peter Dinklage] Yeah,
well I try to, you know.

00:14:04.019 --> 00:14:06.156
And I'm not always successful at it.

00:14:06.156 --> 00:14:07.279
You do have to make a living.

00:14:07.279 --> 00:14:11.687
And I do not fault anyone
else who makes choices

00:14:11.687 --> 00:14:15.854
to play characters that they
wish they hadn't agreed to do.

00:14:17.629 --> 00:14:19.475
'Cause at the end of the day,

00:14:19.475 --> 00:14:23.840
none of us are happy with
our jobs all the time.

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:27.357
But I just sort of, I
had some perseverance,

00:14:27.357 --> 00:14:29.429
in terms of what I wanted to do

00:14:29.429 --> 00:14:30.547
and what I didn't want to do.

00:14:30.547 --> 00:14:34.639
And I think no is a very
powerful word in our business

00:14:34.639 --> 00:14:37.989
that is very hard to use
early on in your career.

00:14:37.989 --> 00:14:42.156
But I also think I was pretty
arrogant when I was younger.

00:14:42.998 --> 00:14:45.599
So I use that word, maybe too much,

00:14:45.599 --> 00:14:47.767
but it also helped me
with the finding roles

00:14:47.767 --> 00:14:49.198
that I did like.

00:14:49.198 --> 00:14:51.509
- [Interviewer] What was the
first time you stood on stage

00:14:51.509 --> 00:14:55.548
and thought, I want to act and
this the only thing I want to do?

00:14:55.548 --> 00:14:57.357
- [Peter Dinklage] Oh,
it was so many years ago.

00:14:57.357 --> 00:14:58.190
I don't remember.

00:14:58.190 --> 00:14:59.729
- [Interviewer] Oh, you're
a baby, give me a break.

00:14:59.729 --> 00:15:01.979
(laughing)

00:15:03.734 --> 00:15:05.048
- [Peter Dinklage] Oh, I think a lot of us

00:15:05.048 --> 00:15:07.894
are just little hams when we're children

00:15:07.894 --> 00:15:09.977
and it just starts that way.

00:15:09.977 --> 00:15:12.516
But, you know, my mom's
a music school teacher

00:15:12.516 --> 00:15:13.825
and kid's would come in,

00:15:13.825 --> 00:15:16.057
it was a revolving door
policy at our house,

00:15:16.057 --> 00:15:17.169
growing up in New Jersey,

00:15:17.169 --> 00:15:19.807
where piano lessons were given
all day throughout the day,

00:15:19.807 --> 00:15:22.797
and my brother and I would
be putting on puppet shows

00:15:22.797 --> 00:15:23.929
down in the basement,

00:15:23.929 --> 00:15:25.749
and constructing these elaborate sets,

00:15:25.749 --> 00:15:30.088
and putting on these shows
for all the octogenarians

00:15:30.088 --> 00:15:31.048
of the neighborhood...

00:15:31.048 --> 00:15:31.881
(laughing)

00:15:31.881 --> 00:15:34.436
that were kind enough to pay a nickel.

00:15:34.436 --> 00:15:35.957
- [Interviewer] So the arts was sort of,

00:15:35.957 --> 00:15:37.143
this was something in
your background already.

00:15:37.143 --> 00:15:38.945
- [Peter Dinklage] It just
came at an early age, yeah.

00:15:38.945 --> 00:15:40.385
I don't know when it starts.

00:15:40.385 --> 00:15:43.043
When you trace it back to
when you were a little child,

00:15:43.043 --> 00:15:45.554
you can't really remember this big moment.

00:15:45.554 --> 00:15:47.713
I think it's a lot of
little moments that build up

00:15:47.713 --> 00:15:50.523
into something that just forms who you are

00:15:50.523 --> 00:15:52.645
and your need to be an actor.

00:15:52.645 --> 00:15:54.334
- Can you just catch me up
on what's happened so far

00:15:54.334 --> 00:15:55.516
in the first few seasons
of Game of Thrones?

00:15:55.516 --> 00:15:56.349
- What?

00:15:56.349 --> 00:15:57.254
We got a minute.

00:15:57.254 --> 00:15:58.403
- We've got 45 seconds now.

00:15:58.403 --> 00:15:59.769
- Stabby, stabby, stabby, stabby.

00:15:59.769 --> 00:16:02.382
Sexy, sexy, sexy, stabby, stabby, stabby.

00:16:02.382 --> 00:16:04.565
Beautiful, beautiful language

00:16:04.565 --> 00:16:08.046
and beautiful scenes of poetry and musing

00:16:08.046 --> 00:16:12.016
on the world as we know
it in King's Landing

00:16:12.016 --> 00:16:13.958
and then a couple jokes.

00:16:13.958 --> 00:16:15.497
(laughing)

00:16:15.497 --> 00:16:16.524
And more stabby, stabby.

00:16:16.524 --> 00:16:17.975
(laughing)

00:16:17.975 --> 00:16:21.225
(making sound effects)

00:16:22.776 --> 00:16:26.326
I think one brother, sister
(making sound effects).

00:16:26.326 --> 00:16:27.159
- Wait, what's that?

00:16:27.159 --> 00:16:27.992
What's that mean?

00:16:27.992 --> 00:16:28.825
(making sound effects)

00:16:28.825 --> 00:16:30.282
They were having parties,
like a tea party?

00:16:30.282 --> 00:16:31.162
- Yeah, they're going,

00:16:31.162 --> 00:16:34.301
"Look at my fingers in a circle.

00:16:34.301 --> 00:16:35.551
"Look at that."

00:16:37.821 --> 00:16:39.244
- Sounds good.
- Yeah, man.

